\chapter{Overview}
\label{ch:overview}

\emph{Majas matematiska äventyr} ([[maventyr]]) is a text adventure in the
spirit of the classic \texttt{adventure} game.  Where \texttt{adventure} hid
treasure behind puzzles, [[maventyr]] hides progress behind \emph{learning}:
the player explores a self-generating labyrinth and advances by solving tasks
drawn from the Swedish school curriculum.  This document is the literate
source --- the program and the reasoning behind it are developed together, so
that a maintainer can read why the game is built the way it is, not merely
what each function does.

This chapter is the maintainer's map.  It states the one architectural rule
that everything else depends on, sketches how the finished system decomposes
into chapters, and then gives the buildable skeleton that the remaining
chapters will grow into.  At this stage (milestone~M0) the package does almost
nothing; its job is to \emph{compile, tangle, and test} cleanly so that every
later chapter has a working scaffold to attach to.

\section{The one rule: the core never speaks to the screen}

Two interfaces are required of [[maventyr]]: a terminal version (the
[[play]] subcommand) and a browser version (the [[serve]] subcommand).  A
program that scatters \texttt{print} and \texttt{input} through its game logic
can support exactly one of these, and bolting on the second means a rewrite.

We therefore adopt a single, load-bearing rule:

\begin{quotation}
  The game core never imports a user interface and never calls \texttt{print}
  or \texttt{input}.
\end{quotation}

The core instead exposes state and a \emph{command\,$\to$\,events} interface:
a frontend turns a player action into a \emph{command}, hands it to the
engine, and renders the \emph{events} the engine returns.  Because the engine
neither reads nor writes the terminal, the terminal frontend and the web
frontend can be two thin translators over the \emph{same} engine.  This rule
is why the decomposition below separates \enquote{core} chapters from
\enquote{interface} chapters, and it is the property each future chapter must
preserve.

\section{How the system decomposes}

The finished game grows one chapter per concern, each in its own \texttt{.nw}
file under \texttt{src/maventyr/} and each corresponding to an epic in the
project's issue tracker.  Grouping the chapters by role makes the dependency
order visible: the core must exist before anything can render or grade, and
content sits on top of all of it.

\begin{description}
  \item[Core] \texttt{entities}, \texttt{locations}, \texttt{maps}, and the
    \texttt{game} engine model the world and the command/event loop.  An
    \emph{entity} is the one structure shared by characters and items; a
    \emph{location} holds entities and links to other locations, possibly in
    another map; a \emph{map} is a labyrinth of locations.
  \item[Mechanics] \texttt{render} draws the discovered map, \texttt{questions}
    grades answers through a per-subject grader (\texttt{sympy} for
    mathematics), and \texttt{persistence} saves and restores the world
    between runs.
  \item[Interfaces] \texttt{ui\_terminal}, \texttt{ui\_web}, and \texttt{cli}
    are the thin frontends and entry point --- the only place \texttt{print},
    HTTP, and argument parsing live.
  \item[Content] \texttt{curriculum} and \texttt{didactics} decide which task
    to pose and how it is authored, and \texttt{content} loads the swappable
    on-disk database whose language sets the game's language.
\end{description}

Each of these is introduced by its own issue and added as a sibling chapter;
the document wrapper in \texttt{doc/} \texttt{\textbackslash input}s them in
reading order.  Keeping this list in step with the chapters that actually
exist is part of the cost of any structural change.

\section{The generated module}

The skeleton below is the whole of the package at milestone~M0.  We assemble
it from named buckets so that later chapters extend the package by adding to a
bucket rather than rewriting a monolith.  At M0 only two buckets carry
content --- the module-level constants and functions; later chapters
introduce an \texttt{imports} bucket and a \texttt{classes} bucket into this
assembly at the point where the first import and the first class actually
appear, so the module never references a bucket that has no content.

