\section{Version}

We expose the package version as a module attribute so that callers --- the
save-format versioning in \texttt{persistence}, a future \texttt{--version}
flag, release tooling --- have a single authoritative source rather than
duplicating the literal.  It mirrors the version declared in
\texttt{pyproject.toml}.

<<constants>>=
__version__ = "0.1"
@

\section{A first, verifiable behaviour}

Even an empty game should be able to greet its player, and we use that
greeting to prove the build pipeline end to end: the chunk below tangles to
Python, the package imports, and the test that follows runs.  The greeting is
a single Swedish constant rather than a literal inside the function, because
the game's default language is Swedish and the eventual content database will
override such strings; keeping the text in a named constant marks it as
translatable rather than incidental.

<<constants>>=
WELCOME = "Välkommen till Majas matematiska äventyr!"
@

<<functions>>=
def greeting():
    """Return the game's welcome message (Swedish by default)."""
    return WELCOME
@

Let us verify that the package imports and that the greeting is the Swedish
welcome --- the smoke test that guards the M0 scaffold.  We read the version
through the module object rather than the star-import, because [[*]] omits
dunder names like [[__version__]]; the public names [[greeting]] and
[[WELCOME]] come in through the star-import as usual.

<<test [[maventyr.py]]>>=
import maventyr
from maventyr import *


def test_has_version():
    assert isinstance(maventyr.__version__, str)


def test_greeting_is_swedish_welcome():
    assert greeting() == WELCOME
    assert "äventyr" in greeting()
@
